<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ojibwe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ojibwa, Chippewa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ojibwe, also called Ojibwa or Chippewa, are an Algonquian-speaking Indigenous people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Allied with French traders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>, they expanded west in the 17th and 18th centuries and pushed the Dakota out of much of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesota">Minnesota</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>. They are one of the largest Indigenous nations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, with many bands and reserves on both sides of the border.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ojibwe.jpg" alt="Ojibwe" width="320" height="355" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ojibwe_Language_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Ojibwe are an Anishinaabe people, and several distinct nations, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saulteaux">Saulteaux</a>, Nipissings and Oji-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a>, also consider themselves Ojibwe. Their name is known in French as Ojibwa and in English as Chippewa, and its meaning is not known. According to Ojibwe oral history and birch bark scrolls, the people originated at the mouth of the Saint Lawrence River on the Atlantic coast of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, and migrated and traded widely across the continent. In their account, seven great miigis, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowrie</a> shells, appeared in the Land of the Dawn to teach the mide way of life, and six of them remained to establish the doodem, or clans. The Ojibwe belong to the Council of Three Fires with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawa">Odawa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potawatomi">Potawatomi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first historical mention of the Ojibwe is in the French Jesuit Relation of 1640. Through friendship with French traders they gained guns and European goods and began to dominate their traditional enemies to the west and south. They drove the Sioux from the Upper Mississippi region towards the present-day Dakotas and forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meskwaki">Meskwaki</a> down from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>. By the end of the 18th century the Ojibwe controlled nearly all of present-day Michigan, northern Wisconsin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesota">Minnesota</a>, including most of the Red River area, as well as the northern shores of lakes Huron and Superior on the Canadian side, westward to the Turtle Mountains of North Dakota.</p><p>Decades of fur trading with the French made their language one of the key trade languages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> and the northern Great Plains. European powers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> have colonised Ojibwe lands, and the Ojibwe signed treaties with settler leaders to share land in exchange for compensation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Ojibwe are organised in bands, and most, except for the Great Plains bands, have historically lived settled lives. They fished and hunted, cultivated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and squash, and harvested manoomin, or wild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. Their typical dwelling is the wiigiwaam, made of birch bark, juniper bark and willow saplings. They are known for birchbark canoes, birchbark scrolls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mining and trade, and the making of maple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>.</p><p>Traditionally kinship followed the father's clan. The Midewiwin society keeps scrolls with pictorial writing that record oral history, songs, maps, geometry and mathematics, and their beliefs have been passed down through its teachings. Their language, Anishinaabemowin or Ojibwemowin, belongs to the Algonquian family. Fluent speakers have declined sharply and are mostly elders, and a movement to revive the language has grown since the early 21st century. The dreamcatcher developed from the Ojibwe spider web charm.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ojibwe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sioux comes from a French corruption of the <b>Ojibwe</b> Nadowessiwak.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lakota-people">Lakota people · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Dennis J. Banks, an <b>Ojibwe</b>, co-founded AIM in Minneapolis in 1968 and became one of its most recognisable faces.</blockquote><a href="/movement/american-indian-movement">American Indian Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The words have been translated into Inuktitut, <b>Ojibwe</b>, Cree and Mi'kmaq, and there is a trilingual version in English, French and Inuktitut; Daniel Tlen sang it in Southern Tutchone at the opening of the 1988 Winter Olympics in Calgary, and in February 2007 Akina Shirt became the first…</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-canada">O Canada</a></li><li><blockquote>The United States proclaimed the Treaty of St. Louis with the Council of Three Fires, the Ottawa, <b>Ojibwe</b> and Potawatomi of the Illinois and Milwaukee rivers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-30">30 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During a game of baaga'adowe, a form of lacrosse, <b>Ojibwe</b> players chased the ball into the fort and took it by surprise.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-2">2 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Treaties with the eastern Dakota and <b>Ojibwe</b> gradually forced them onto reservations.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-11">11 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
